        <div id="fridge-watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Watts Does a Refrigerator Use? Around 150 Running, Far More to Start
          </h2>

          <p>
            A refrigerator is one of the easiest appliances to back up because it uses little power on average. The catch is the moment the compressor starts. That surge, not the running draw, is what decides how big a generator you need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running Watts</h3>

          <p>
            A modern full-size refrigerator typically draws somewhere around 100 to 250 watts while the compressor is running, and nothing at all while it rests. Older models, side-by-sides and units with failing door seals can draw more. Over a day, most modern fridges use roughly 1 to 2 kWh, which is the number that matters if you are considering a battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Watts</h3>

          <p>
            When the compressor kicks on it can pull several times its running draw for a second or two. Many fridges surge somewhere in the 800 to 1,500-watt range, and an older unit can be higher. A generator that cannot supply that burst trips its overload protection or stalls the compressor, which is bad for the motor. The yellow EnergyGuide label gives annual energy use, but the surge is rarely printed, so size with margin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Defrost Heater</h3>

          <p>
            Frost-free refrigerators run an electric defrost heater a few times a day, typically several hundred watts for up to half an hour. If the defrost cycle and a freezer compressor overlap, the load jumps. It is one more reason not to size a generator right to the edge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chest and Upright Freezers</h3>

          <p>
            A chest freezer usually draws less than a refrigerator and holds temperature much longer, because cold air does not spill out when you open the lid. An upright freezer with an auto-defrost heater behaves more like a fridge. Count each freezer's surge separately.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
