        <div id="food-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Long Does Food Last Without Power? About 4 Hours in the Fridge, 48 in a Full Freezer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The USDA's guidance sets your deadline. Knowing it tells you how urgently the generator needs to go on, and how long you can leave it off once things are cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Refrigerator: About 4 Hours</h3>

          <p>
            A closed refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours without power. After that, perishable food such as meat, poultry, seafood, milk, eggs and leftovers that has been above 40°F for more than 2 hours should be thrown out. Keep the doors closed; every opening dumps cold air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Freezer: 24 to 48 Hours</h3>

          <p>
            A full freezer holds its temperature for about 48 hours if the door stays closed, and a half-full one for about 24 hours. Frozen food that still has ice crystals or reads 40°F or below can be refrozen. Filling empty freezer space with jugs of water before storm season buys you extra hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use a Thermometer, Not a Guess</h3>

          <p>
            An appliance thermometer in both compartments removes the guesswork. The fridge should sit at or below 40°F and the freezer at 0°F, as explained in our guide to <a href="/what-temperature-should-a-refrigerator-be/">what temperature a refrigerator should be</a>. When in doubt, throw it out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cycling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Need to Run the Generator All Day? No, Cycle the Fridge
          </h2>

          <p>
            A refrigerator does not need continuous power. Because a closed fridge holds a safe temperature for hours, you can run the generator in blocks and save a great deal of fuel and noise.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Simple Schedule</h3>

          <p>
            A common approach is to run the fridge and freezer for one to two hours, then shut the generator off for three to four hours, keeping the doors closed in between. The compressors run hard during the on period and pull the temperature down, and the insulated cabinets coast through the off period. Watch the thermometers and adjust: if the fridge climbs toward 40°F before the next run, shorten the gap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Overnight</h3>

          <p>
            Many people run the generator in the evening, shut it off for the night and start it again in the morning. A full freezer will not notice. The fridge may creep a few degrees, which is fine if it started cold. This keeps you from refuelling in the dark and spares the neighbors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Let It Settle Before Restarting</h3>

          <p>
            If a fridge compressor is stopped mid-cycle, give it a few minutes before restarting it, because a compressor trying to start against high pressure draws a larger surge. Plugging the fridge in after the generator is running and stable, rather than starting the generator under load, is gentler on both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter-vs-conventional" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Inverter or Conventional Generator for a Refrigerator? Inverter, for Three Reasons
          </h2>

          <p>
            A conventional open-frame generator will physically run most refrigerators. We still recommend an inverter for this job, and every gas pick in this guide is one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Modern Fridges Have Electronics</h3>

          <p>
            Most current refrigerators have electronic control boards, and many use variable-speed inverter compressors. Inverter generators are typically rated under 3% total harmonic distortion, close to grid power. Some conventional generators produce far more distorted power, and owners commonly report fridge boards misbehaving on it. Our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel Use at Light Load</h3>

          <p>
            A conventional generator runs its engine at full speed regardless of load. An inverter throttles down to match demand. A fridge is a small, intermittent load, which is exactly where an inverter's fuel savings are largest, so a gallon goes much further.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Noise</h3>

          <p>
            Fridge backup usually means running for hours near the house and the neighbors. A 2,000-watt inverter is far quieter than an open-frame generator of similar output. See our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generators</a> for more models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gas Generator or Battery Power Station for a Fridge?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery power stations have become a real option for refrigerator backup. They suit some households better than a generator, and the best setup often uses both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Run Time Math</h3>

          <p>
            Divide the station's capacity by the fridge's daily use, then allow for inverter losses. A 2kWh station like the Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 runs a typical fridge for roughly a day. A 1kWh station like the EcoFlow DELTA 3 manages around half that. Our guide to whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">a portable power station can run a refrigerator</a> goes deeper on the numbers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Batteries Win</h3>

          <p>
            Stations make no exhaust and no noise, so they can sit in the kitchen beside the fridge. That makes them the right answer for apartments, condos and homes without a safe outdoor spot for a generator. They need no fuel storage or oil changes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Gas Wins</h3>

          <p>
            A generator runs as long as you have fuel, so it wins outright in a multi-day outage. It also costs far less per hour of backup. See <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">portable power station vs generator</a> for a full comparison.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Using Both</h3>

          <p>
            The most comfortable setup pairs a small inverter generator with a fast-charging station. Run the generator for an hour or two in the day to cool the fridge and refill the battery, then switch the fridge to the station and shut the engine off for a silent night.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connect-safely" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Connect a Generator to a Refrigerator Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            A refrigerator has a plug, so connecting it is simpler than a hardwired furnace. Simple is not the same as careless, though.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Right Extension Cord</h3>

          <p>
            Use an outdoor-rated, three-prong heavy-duty cord, ideally 12-gauge for runs up to about 50 feet, and never a light indoor cord. An undersized cord drops voltage, which makes compressors work harder and run hot. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size</a> guide has a full table.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Consider an Inlet Box Later</h3>

          <p>
            If you find yourself running cords to several appliances every outage, an inlet box with an interlock kit or transfer switch feeds the kitchen circuits directly. That needs a generator with a suitable outlet and an electrician. Never backfeed through a wall or dryer outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Carbon Monoxide Outside</h3>

          <p>
            Run the generator outdoors at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, and never in a garage. Every gas pick here has a CO shutdown, but you still need battery-powered carbon monoxide alarms inside. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers rain and snow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Fuel to Keep a Fridge Cold for Three Days
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel planning for a fridge generator is modest, which is part of the appeal of the small inverters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gasoline</h3>

          <p>
            A 2,000-watt inverter running a fridge at light load uses roughly a tank a day if run continuously, and much less if you cycle it. The Honda EU2200i holds 0.95 gallons, rated for 3.2 to 8.1 hours. Three days of cycled running is realistic on 5 gallons of stabilized gasoline in an approved can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane</h3>

          <p>
            Propane stores for years and the dual fuel picks run happily on standard 20-pound grill tanks. The Champion 4000 lists up to 25 hours on propane. Two or three full tanks cover a long weekend outage with margin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Ready</h3>

          <p>
            Start the generator every month or two and run it under load for a few minutes. Drain or stabilize gasoline before storage, and use the fuel shut-off where the generator has one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by Running Watts and Ignoring the Surge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fridge drawing 150 watts can pull well over 1,000 watts for a second as the compressor starts. Buying a tiny generator or power station based on running watts alone leads to tripped overloads and stalled compressors. Size for the start-up surge of every compressor you plan to run, then add a margin for the defrost heater and lights.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Generator in the Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage door does not clear carbon monoxide, and the gas seeps into the house. Generator CO poisoning kills people every storm season. Run it outdoors at least 20 feet from the house with exhaust pointed away from openings, and keep battery-powered CO alarms working inside, even if the generator has an automatic shutoff.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening the Fridge Every Time You Check It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each opening dumps cold air and shortens the safe window. Put appliance thermometers where you can read them quickly, plan meals so you open the doors once, and move drinks to a cooler. A closed fridge and a full freezer buy you hours of generator-off time and save fuel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Thin Indoor Extension Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A long, light-gauge cord drops voltage, which makes the compressor run hot and struggle to start. Use an outdoor-rated, three-prong heavy-duty cord sized for the run, ideally 12-gauge up to about 50 feet, and keep connections out of puddles and snow.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Gasoline in the Generator Between Outages</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline left in the tank and carburetor goes stale and gums up the jets, which is the most common reason emergency generators refuse to start. Use the fuel shut-off to run the carburetor dry, add stabilizer, or choose a dual fuel model and store propane instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

