        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best generator for a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes the Honda EU2200i is the best generator for a refrigerator: quiet, clean inverter power that Honda specifically lists for refrigerators, plus CO-MINDER shutdown. The Westinghouse iGen2550c is the value pick with more surge headroom. If you want propane, choose the WEN DF360iX. For indoor, fume-free backup, the Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 battery station runs a fridge for roughly a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need to run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2,000 to 2,500-watt inverter generator is the practical minimum for a full-size refrigerator. The fridge only draws around 100 to 250 watts running, but its compressor can surge to several times that at start-up. To add a chest freezer, choose 2,500 to 3,600 starting watts so both compressors can start without tripping the generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 2000-watt generator run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, a quality 2,000-watt inverter runs most kitchen refrigerators, and usually a small freezer too. The key is surge capacity: the compressor briefly pulls far more than its running watts. Plug the fridge in after the generator is running, avoid starting other large loads at the same moment, and keep microwaves and heaters off the same generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a refrigerator go without power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                According to USDA guidance, a closed refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours. A full freezer holds for about 48 hours and a half-full one for about 24 hours if the door stays closed. Discard perishable food that has been above 40°F for more than 2 hours, and use appliance thermometers to check.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I run a generator for my refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You do not need to run it continuously. A common pattern is one to two hours on, then three to four hours off with the doors closed, checking that the fridge stays at or below 40°F. Many people run it in the evening and morning and leave it off overnight, which saves fuel and noise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an inverter generator for a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is strongly recommended. Modern refrigerators use electronic control boards and often variable-speed compressors, which prefer the clean power inverters provide, typically under 3% THD. Inverters also throttle down at light loads like a fridge, which saves a lot of fuel, and they are much quieter for overnight running near the house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A fridge typically uses 1 to 2 kWh a day, so a 2kWh station runs one for roughly a day and a 1kWh station for around half that. It needs enough AC output to handle the compressor's start-up surge. Stations are ideal indoors because they make no exhaust, and they pair well with a small generator for recharging.
              </p>
            </div>

